  "rationale": "Fever plus a non-blanching rash should be treated as meningococcaemia until proven otherwise.",
  "optionExplanations": {
    "A": "Pneumococcaemia can cause severe sepsis, but meningococcaemia is the classic and most concerning cause of fever with a non-blanching rash.",
    "B": "Viral illnesses commonly cause blanching rashes; a non-blanching rash is more concerning for serious bacterial infection or haematological disease.",
    "C": "Henoch-Schönlein purpura can cause purpura, but fever with a non-blanching rash is more immediately concerning for invasive infection.",
    "D": "A coagulopathy may contribute to purpura, but meningococcaemia is the most urgent diagnosis to consider in this presentation.",
    "E": "Correct. Meningococcaemia is a life-threatening cause of fever and non-blanching rash and must be treated as an emergency."
  },
